The Move-native LED strip in shadow_led_queue.c was hardcoded to a
specific tool's needs — it stripped pads + every non-master CC Move
sent during co-run, regardless of what the tool had actually declared
in its keep_mask. That baked one consumer's keep set into the
framework.

Generalize: iterate Move's cable-0 CC / note-on / note-off LED writes,
map each to a CORUN_GRP_* via the existing corun_group_for_event()
helper, and strip iff the group is in the tool's keep_mask. Surfaces
the tool cedes pass through naturally — Move's LEDs reach hardware
for whatever it owns. Sysex LED writes (status 0xF0-0xF7) and
unclassified events aren't group-mapped today, so they fall through
unchanged; sysex specifically carries Move's palette colors for the
knob rings + master (idx 71-79), which the framework intentionally
leaves alone.

Net effect: LED ownership now mirrors input routing (tool keeps a
group = tool owns input AND LED; cedes = peer owns both). No tool can
push a specific LED ownership policy onto the framework without
declaring the corresponding keep_mask.

docs/CORUN.md gets a short "LED ownership" subsection under the
Move-firmware coupling discussion.
